# --- Hem configuration -------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env and edit. Every value below is optional; the defaults match a
# stock Hermes install on this machine.

# Port the LAN-facing proxy listens on (this is what you open from the phone).
PROXY_PORT=3000

# Interface the proxy binds. 0.0.0.0 = reachable from the phone on your LAN.
PROXY_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Where the Hermes backend lives. Keep it on loopback: Hermes forces OAuth on
# a non-loopback bind, and its Host/Origin guards check the bound address.
HERMES_HOST=127.0.0.1
HERMES_PORT=9119

# Dashboard session token used to authenticate against the Hermes backend.
#
# Leave EMPTY to auto-discover: the proxy scrapes the token that Hermes injects
# into its own SPA HTML (GET / on loopback). That works against a `hermes
# dashboard` or `hermes serve` you did not start yourself.
#
# Set it explicitly when start.sh launches Hermes for you — it is exported as
# HERMES_DASHBOARD_SESSION_TOKEN so the backend adopts this exact value.
HERMES_TOKEN=

# --- Optional: the address other devices should use --------------------------
# Only needed when something in front of the proxy owns the public address —
# `tailscale serve`, say. It's what the QR code and the install hint hand out.
# TAILSCALE=1 bash start.sh sets this for you.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://your-host.your-tailnet.ts.net

# --- Optional: the public endpoint (Cloudflare Tunnel + Access) ---------------
#
# These belong in a SEPARATE file, `.env.public`, layered on top of this one:
#     ENV_FILE=.env.public bash start.sh
# That keeps the LAN config here intact, so switching deployments is a flag at
# launch rather than a diff to apply and reverse. See the README.
#
# For publishing on a real domain instead of a tailnet. `cloudflared` runs on
# this machine and dials out to Cloudflare, so no router port is opened and the
# home IP never appears in DNS. Cloudflare Access runs the Google sign-in at the
# edge and refuses everyone outside ACCESS_ALLOWED_EMAILS before a packet gets
# here.
#
# Setting all three ALSO makes this proxy verify Access's signed assertion
# itself, on every request and every WebSocket upgrade. That is what makes a
# tunnel pointed at the wrong port, or a stray client on the LAN, fail closed
# rather than quietly handing over the agent. Leave any of them empty and the
# gate is off entirely, which is what dev, LAN and Tailscale want.
#
# With the gate on, set PROXY_HOST=127.0.0.1 above: cloudflared reaches the
# proxy over loopback, and closing the LAN port removes the one way in that
# Access cannot see.
#
# ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN is your Zero Trust team hostname; ACCESS_AUD is the
# Application Audience tag from the Access application's Overview tab.
# ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com
# ACCESS_AUD=0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
# ACCESS_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@gmail.com
# PUBLIC_URL=https://hermes.example.com

# --- Optional: HTTPS ---------------------------------------------------------
# Set BOTH to serve over TLS, which lights up the dormant PWA layer (installable
# app, service worker, offline reading, web push). Generate with mkcert:
#   mkcert -install
#   mkcert -cert-file certs/lan.pem -key-file certs/lan-key.pem <LAN-IP> localhost
# HTTPS_CERT=./certs/lan.pem
# HTTPS_KEY=./certs/lan-key.pem

# --- Optional: web push ------------------------------------------------------
# Push works with no configuration once the app is served over HTTPS: a VAPID
# keypair is generated on first boot and stored in .hermes-push.json next to
# this file, alongside the subscribed devices.
#
# Set these only to pin a keypair — moving the app to another machine, say.
# Changing the public key invalidates every existing subscription, so each
# phone has to re-enable notifications in Settings afterwards.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Contact address the push service can reach you at. mailto: or https:.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# Set to 0 to switch push off entirely, including the gateway socket the proxy
# otherwise holds open to watch for events worth notifying about.
PUSH_ENABLED=1

# Log level: trace | debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info
